cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "anchorkey: auditable provenance for observed-entity telemetry under identifier drift"
abstract: >-
  A small, dependency-free framework for earning a stable identity for IoT and
  observed-entity telemetry: deterministic natural-key normalization under identifier
  drift, plus an entity-resolution layer that merges only what normalization proves
  identical and defers every other pair to an auditable, effective-dated human label.
  Fuzzy auto-merge is deliberately absent rather than unimplemented: five string-similarity
  metrics were measured against datasheet-verified ground truth and all of them merge
  genuinely different parts at any threshold loose enough to catch genuine typos, because
  the deciding fact is a manufacturer's catalog external to the string. Immutable-snapshot
  integrity is forthcoming with a later paper. The reference implementation for a technical
  paper series on surrogate-key failure under identifier drift.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Saha
    given-names: Rahul
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-8172-0676"
version: "0.2.0"
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - IoT
  - telemetry
  - identity resolution
  - entity resolution
  - data quality
  - natural key
repository-code: "https://github.com/saharah-99/anchorkey"

# NOTE ON DOIs, because two different things are citable here and conflating them is wrong.
#
# The Zenodo record below is typed *Dataset*: it archives the openSenseMap capture and the
# analysis scripts. It is NOT a DOI for this software, so it must not appear as a top-level
# `doi:` here -- that would tell anyone citing anchorkey to cite the dataset instead.
#
# The software has no DOI yet. Once the GitHub-Zenodo integration archives a tagged release,
# add the minted software DOI as a top-level field:
#     doi: "10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXXX"

